        "@tbl-strategy-performance and @fig-strategy-comparison compare the average out-of-sample Sharpe ratios across portfolio strategies and sample sizes. The true optimum is included as a benchmark representing the highest Sharpe ratio attainable when the true parameters are known.\n",
        "\n",
        "The left part of the figure displays the estimation-robust strategies, namely the equally weighted portfolio and the inverse-volatility portfolio. Both strategies achieve Sharpe ratios close to the true optimum and remain almost unchanged as the sample size increases. This indicates that their performance is largely unaffected by estimation error because they rely little or not at all on estimated parameters.\n",
        "\n",
        "The right part of the figure displays the strategies that rely more directly on estimated moments. The plug-in tangency portfolio performs poorly when $T$ is small because it depends on estimates of both expected returns and the covariance matrix. The global minimum variance portfolio performs better because it avoids estimating expected returns, but it is still affected by estimation error in the covariance matrix. The long-only tangency portfolio improves on the unconstrained plug-in tangency portfolio by preventing extreme short positions, which makes it less sensitive to noisy estimates.\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, the results suggest that the equally weighted and inverse-volatility portfolios are the most robust to estimation error, while the unconstrained plug-in tangency portfolio is the most sensitive. The long-only tangency portfolio shows that imposing practical constraints can mitigate some of the estimation error, although simple portfolio rules remain more stable in this simulation design.\n",
        "\n",
        "A practical lesson for investors is therefore that highly optimized portfolios may perform poorly out-of-sample when the inputs are estimated with noise. Simple or constrained strategies can be preferable in realistic settings where the true parameters are unknown and only limited historical data is available."
